{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Built the event that makes the liveness measurement non-vacuous, and recorded the one real failure it cost. The per-step persist/apply pass now skips a randomly chosen node about one time in six, so a committed configuration change can genuinely stay unapplied at that node; the draft-side run then reports unapplied_conf_change_states=48 against stalled_states=0, meaning at all 48 check points where a node held a committed-but-unapplied change at least one node was still promotable - a descriptive liveness observation rather than the earlier uninterpretable zero. The first version of this revision panicked with '1 state.commit 5 is out of range [0, 4]' from loadState at a restart event, because a node whose application lagged could hold a committed index beyond the entries its storage had received, making its hard state inadmissible to newRaft; flushing that node's committed entries to storage before recording the hard state fixed it, and that ordering is exactly the application duty the Storage contract implies - a useful by-product of introducing lag. The safety counters still hold with lag present: 262 configuration changes, 429 restarts, 470 elections, 461 ordinary proposals, violations=0. Because these numbers are construction feedback only, the submission requests isolated execution of these bytes, describing the lag event, the flush-before-hardstate fix, and the witness instrumentation that emits per-node operands (state, term, applied, committed, has_unapplied, promotable, in_config) if the witness fires. The still-open liveness question now has a runnable shape: the receipt must show unapplied_conf_change_states non-zero before its stalled_states value can be read, and the safety half's inherited limits (between-events checks, term rather than full-identity comparison, no storage faults, no demotion of an existing serving voter) remain as recorded.\"}"}}
